  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM EST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nHigh pressure remains in control. The result is above normal \nafternoon temperatures and dry weather across the region. The most \nimpactful weather element will be the low afternoon relative \nhumidity values. These low RH values, combined with ongoing drought \nacross the area, may lead to an increase in a few wildfires over the \nnext few days. Thankfully the winds will remain light though. Due to \nthe dry air, NBM dewpoints and temps have been off. NBM10 for \ndewpoints were used over the next few days as these have been closer \nto observations. Also lowered overnight lows several degrees areawide, \nand bumped up highs for tomorrow. This is normal when models don't \nhave a handle on how dry the air is. \n\nOn Saturday, an area of low pressure is forecast to move along the \nCarolina coast and then inland into NC and VA. At this time, it \nappears that this system will stay far enough east to keep us dry \nas the western edge of the precip shield should stay just on the \nother side of the Appalachians. The only thing to watch with this \nsystem will be the winds. For now, winds don't look to worrisome. \nHowever, if the system tracks slightly further west we will see the \nhigher wind-field nudge into are area. These higher winds and lower \nRH values would be a concern for any ongoing or new fires across the \narea. The main areas to watch will be the east TN and southwest VA \nmountains. \n\nA weak front moves in Sunday night and brings chances for light rain \ninto portions of the area. Most areas stay dry, but for those who do \nget rain, amounts will generally only be a few hundredths of an inch.\n\nCooler temps and dry weather expected Monday through Tuesday behind \nthe frontal passage with temperatures back down closer to seasonal \nnormals. Our next chance of precip comes Wed/Thu as an upper trough \napproaches from the northwest and a system in the Gulf approaches \nfrom the south.",
